[{:seon.task/name "a-process-says-what-it-is"
  :seon.task/plan "In Activity Monitor and ps, every process a root forks (the fork server, its standby, each agent, clone and test child) shows the root's own command line, ./seon root PORT, so the owner can't tell a root from an agent or which checkout it belongs to (14 processes across five checkouts, all named alike). Correct: each process names itself by role and checkout when it starts, e.g. \"seon fork-server (next-wiki)\", \"seon agent a3 (next-wiki)\": on macOS by rewriting its argv area in place (what setproctitle does), so ps shows it; the same name is a fact on its entity. Part of the process principle (docs/processes.md#the-agent-process). Proof: after spawning an agent, ps -o command= for each pid names its role."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/after-spawning-agent-ps-o-command-pid-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-death-names-its-cause"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: two runs ended `:closed` with reason \"agent a9 died\" and nothing more, while root's log held the causes: an uncaught `{:type :fatal-commit-error}` from Datahike in `seon.repl/record!`, and a ClassCastException (nil cannot be cast to IFn) in `seon.agent/run-tests-forked`, plausibly after the agent redefined a platform function its own loop calls (a real REPL may; the merge judges). What exists now: root's `seon.system/end-agent` makes the death an error `:seon.agent/died` carrying the exit status, the tail of the agent's stderr file (`seon.agent/stderr-path`, `:seon.config.root/death-stderr-bytes`) and its last op, and a throw in a request's step is answered with the error (`seon.repl/defect-answer`, `seon.agent-test/a-step-that-throws-answers-its-error-and-the-agent-goes-on`). What is still missing: an exception that ends the process outside a request's step leaves only what the stderr tail happens to hold. Correct: the agent process's entrant boundary (`seon.fork-server/main` for a child, the agent's serve loop) writes the uncaught exception as the run's end, the one error map (`:seon.error/type`, `:seon.error/operation`, message, cause chain) on its branch, before the process exits; root's judge carries it to the run's reason and evidence. Then find the fatal commit's cause from that evidence."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-step-that-throws-answers-its-error-and-the-agent-goes-on}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-reports-its-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed by worktree session agent-view (2026-10-23): the owner asked the agent's page to show its process's threads and uptime. The agent's :stats answer (seon.agent/handle! :stats) and its hello carry pid, memory and t, no thread count; root's view entry has no start stamp (the agent has :seon.agent/started-ms, which the page shows). Correct: the agent reports its OS thread count with its memory (a fact measured, as mem-mb is), root's view keeps it, and the page's head shows it; the head shows the run's start instead of a ticking uptime (a clock deciding a render). Checked 2026-11-06: no thread count in seon.agent or seon.agent-proc; the named proof ``seon.agent-test/an-agent-reports-its-threads`` is not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-reports-its-threads}}
 {:seon.task/name "fork-error-names-the-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Done in part: `seon.lib/fork!` forks only while this is the one active Chez thread and otherwise calls its :refused fn at once with the active thread count, so a test run beside a thread answers :seon.test/fork-refused {:active-threads n} (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`) instead of the old safe-fork's 1 s spin. Left: `seon.agent`'s interrupt-self-test spins up to 100,000 evals waiting for its own SIGUSR1. Correct: the self-test waits on one safe point (one engine tick) and records whether the handler ran. Deletes the 100,000. Its two proofs are not yet written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-fork-beside-a-thread-is-refused-at-once-naming-it
                      seon.agent-test/the-interrupt-self-test-needs-one-safe-point}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-green-under-the-runner-is-green-in-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by a worked example (evaluations session, 2026-10-23): seon.repl-test/an-error-in-an-anonymous-fn-is-in-the-fn-around-it and seon.repl-test/an-eval-error-says-where-in-its-forms-own-text are green under bin/test on main (cb77a911), but red when run with clojure.test/test-vars at any agent's REPL, on main or on a branch (6 assertions: :seon.error/at and :seon.error/in differ, e.g. {:line 1} for {:line 3}, rewrite-clj.zip.seqz/map-seq for nil); the judge's fork of the agent ran the first red too, and the done rule held the example's run for it as a stray red (with seon.schema-test/register-validates-against-the-population). A test whose verdict depends on where it runs judges nothing: an agent's branch is held for a red its change did not cause. Find the difference (the agent loads its program from the branch's recorded forms, the runner from files: an error's position and the frames Jolt reports may differ), fix it at its producer, so a test's verdict in an agent and its fork equals the runner's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/seon-repl-test-eval-error-says-where-forms}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-first-read-costs-what-a-later-one-does"
  :seon.task/plan "A fresh agent's first request build (seon.turn/context-block) cost about 126M ticks on a worktree root of 908 tasks, against 22-33M for a later one, with main's reconciler index handed by the fork server: its first observe 50M against about 8M warm, its first render about 40M more than a later one, the same calls each time. The cause was first reads of the store: seon.db/after-fork! forgot the parent's connections and with them their node caches, so the agent's own connect started an empty cache and fetched and decoded every node it read (a first seon.problems/caused-split: 487 misses, 1,436 nodes decoded, 38.9M ticks against 7.2M warm, about 65k a miss). Now after-fork! keeps each node cache alone (decoded values at content addresses), and the agent's first connect adopts it (datahike.connections/reserve-connection-opening! shares a cache by node store and threshold): measured on fresh agents, the first observe 21.6M (3 misses) and the first render no dearer than a later one (33.8M against 32.6M); a larger task's whole first read 79M against 61.5M later. Left: about 14M of the first observe is still first-time work that is no store read (seon.problems/caused-split reads sections, var names and catch forms the fork server never decoded: 20.4M against 4.6M, with base and head equal), and the spawn's own key pull, now gone (root names the key). Not yet proved: that the whole first read costs what a later one does, which needs a process root per size (an agent-test cost proof). seon.db-test/a-fork-keeps-its-parents-node-caches-and-no-connection, named below, is green on main and covers what is done, not what is left."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-fork-keeps-its-parents-node-caches-and-no-connection}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-running-fibers-dump-shows-its-current-frames"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-27 (the turn-0 hang): seon's fiber dump (kill -INFO) of a process whose only fiber is running answers 'running fiber, no saved continuation' and names nothing, so the one process that burned 175e9 ticks could not be located in code; its native sample showed only getcwd and directory listing. A running fiber's current frames must be in the dump (Jolt's runtime: the fiber's own stack at the signal, as a parked fiber's saved continuation shows), so the dump of a spinning process names the Clojure function it is in. Belongs to Jolt (a jolt/<topic> branch: docs/forks.md); owner jolt-compare. Proof: a process spinning in a known function (a loop in a test fixture) dumps that function under its running fiber. The named proof ``seon.agent-test/a-dump-of-a-spinning-process-names-its-function`` is not written (no deftest of that name in test/)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-dump-of-a-spinning-process-names-its-function}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-spawns-start-costs-the-same-on-a-bigger-program"
  :seon.task/plan "Red on main before the 2026-10-28 batch and taken out of seon.agent-test then (the owner's rule: a red of unknown cause the batch did not make leaves its test, named here): an agent spawned after main gained 200 forms has a :state phase of 616,608 bytes against 460,304 before, over the bound 1.1x + 64 KiB. A spawn's own work must not grow with main's program (the owner, 2026-10-25). Find what the :state phase reads or builds per form (seon.agent/spawn-phases; the ctx record's pull, the reach graph or index handed at fork, the branch views), fix it at the cause, and restore the deftest from git (it is a-spawns-start-costs-the-same-on-a-bigger-program in test/seon/agent_test.clj at 139bbb75ff). Checked 2026-11-06: no deftest of that name is in test/ (91ac5dfe34 removed it on 2026-10-28; its text is in git); `seon.fork-server-cost-test` holds a cost proof of `seon.fork-server/graph-at-fork` flat in the program. Whether the :state phase still grows was not run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/agent-spawned-after-main-gained-200-forms-has}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-budget-comes-from-measured-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: a budget where 90%+ of the time it's under budget and the rare overrun is mostly a time we should intervene (docs/decisions.md). What exists: an eval's tick and byte budgets are fixed config (:seon.config.agent/form-ticks 20 G, :seon.config.agent/form-bytes 1 GiB), applied by seon.repl to the origins seon.repl/budgeted names (:model, :outside, :ui), counted by seon.intr/run; a test's tick budget is derived from its own history (seon.fx/test-budgets: min(cap, max(floor, factor x the median of its green ticks))). The fixed byte budget stopped root's whole-program loads (fixed: root's evals unbudgeted), an eval's (my.agent/prove) and seon.agent-test/a-test-childs-ceiling-is-its-own. Plan: (1) measure the population first: every recorded agent eval's :ticks and :bytes by origin, from the md-scan bench store's run branches (main holds only root's own evals: 32 records, 2026-10-30), p50, p90, p99, max; (2) one budget function for tests and evals, budget-of [samples {:quantile :factor :floor :cap}] (the per-test budget is the case quantile 0.5 over one test's greens), seon.fx/test-budgets calling it; (3) an eval's budget per origin is budget-of over the recorded evals of that origin (quantile and factor so that 90%+ of past evals pass, config keys with their provenance), derived where an agent is forked (root, from the population at t; derived data, regenerated when evicted), the fixed keys becoming the cap; (4) an overrun stays the typed error to the agent (:seon.repl/ticks-spent, :seon.repl/bytes-spent) and is recorded as a fact, an intervention point root can show. Open: the budget's unit of scope (per eval now; per fiber once the agent's intake dispatcher runs a request on its own fiber: the context session's measurement of whether Jolt fibers share the per-thread counter). Proofs to write: budget-of's cost and quantile behavior; an eval within the derived budget runs; one past it is the typed error; the test budgets unchanged (same numbers before and after)."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/eval-s-tick-byte-budget-origin-budget-over}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reader-of-an-agents-branch-sees-what-its-answered-evals-wrote"
  :seon.task/plan "seon.repl-test/an-edit-in-an-agent-is-program-on-its-branch went red once in batch 66's merge prove (2026-10-31, at ff5f74f128; a 44-test shard of process tests) and green alone twice at the same tree: the agent answered its edit! (:edited), then root's eval read the agent's branch (seon.system/branch-db) and its :var/form for the edited var held the old source, \"[] 2\" missing. So an eval's answer can reach its asker before the branch commit that holds what the eval wrote is readable by another process (seon.repl's serve loop commits after the answer when :dirty?, agent/commit!). A write is answered when committed in memory (docs/storage.md#durability-is-continuous-group-commit), and one followed by an outside effect waits for the sync that covers it; an outside reader acting on an answer is such an effect. Reproduce first: repeat the test's three steps in one shard with load beside it, and count. Correct: what an answered eval wrote is on the branch any reader opens after the answer (commit before answering, or the answer carries the commit and branch-db waits for it); no wait by clock. The proof named below is green on main almost every run (it was red once in a shard of 44), so green is no evidence here; the reproduction is the proof to write."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-edit-in-an-agent-is-program-on-its-branch}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-done-rule-holds-under-load"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-02 in the optimization session's bin/prove --merge of jolt/one-search at load 15-19 (logs target/prove/20261102T110219-57930/shard-2.log): seon.agent-test/a-task-whose-proofs-pass-is-done-only-when-what-it-reaches-is-green answered [:green :merged []], the change that breaks main's edit test merged with no reason named; the same test alone at load ~8 is green (refused, the stray red named). In the same run seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-reads-the-index-it-inherited measured a first grep 9.07 MB against a second 2.38 MB (green alone): seon.profile/alloc-bytes counts the whole process, so a fiber working beside the measured call is counted. Cause of the first not yet known. Ruled out 2026-11-02: the stall bound (the test with :seon.config.agent/stall-ms 1 at load 12 still refused the change, green). Left: a verdict reused from the shared verdict cache (target/results) at a digest the agent's change should have moved, or a reached test the agent's run never ran under load (its test child's ceiling, a test plan that changed); the next look reads that run's store (its root's runs dir) before the test's tree is removed. Correct: whatever the load, a reached test the change breaks is named and the task does not merge (no clock decides: docs/clocks.md#if-nothing-has-changed-we-do-nothing); a cost test measures its own call's allocation, or reads no fiber's beside it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-task-whose-proofs-pass-is-done-only-when-what-it-reaches-is-green}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-agents-state-is-declared"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by docs/merging.md#a-landing-message-carries-its-audit its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: seon.agent/state is one mutable map, its keys described in a comment, its schema the tag :seon.agent/state [:map], with about 62 swap!/reset! sites and no validator (the one set-validator! in src is seon.ctx's). Nothing declares which keys an eval may replace, which survive a fork or which a test child resets, and four fixes were this class: d1ab45ec12 and 24b5508112 (socket and intake reader lost when state was replaced, moved by hand to link), c4a4cdd495 and 2c3867e897 (a test child inherited the interrupt and the page walk, reset by hand at one fork site), 42854c8c33 (a carry read :db from state). The change: :seon.agent/state a closed map with each key's type and a per-key property (:seon.agent/on-fork :reset or :keep); set-validator! from it while contracts are on; the forked child's state derived from the schema in place of the hand dissoc and resets; host handles stay out (in link). Check seon.intr's stack and pending atoms the same way. Docs: docs/agent.md. The named proof ``seon.agent-test/a-forked-childs-state-follows-its-schema`` is not written (no deftest of that name in test/)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-forked-childs-state-follows-its-schema}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-exit-watch-is-an-answer"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-04 in bin/check scale at 100 agents (target/check run of branch jolt/an-evals-record-has-its-shape, 76ab975c96): one agent's test child failed with `jolt.io-poller/exit-chan: kevent refused the watch`, ex-data {:pid 4300 :errno 35} (EAGAIN), thrown at jolt/io_poller.clj under seon.agent/read-to-exit, and went on as `Exception in go/fiber body (channel closed)`: :every-eval-and-test-passed false, 99 of 100 ok. What allowed it: an outside-world failure point (a kernel watch on a child) that throws in a fiber instead of answering, against docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs (a wait ends with its value or a typed error). Correct: find what EAGAIN means for EVFILT_PROC at that moment (Jolt's io poller; the fix is there, on a jolt/<topic> branch, with its gates): retried by what the kernel says, or the exit read another way; and a watch the kernel keeps refusing answers the waiter as an error value the test child reports. Named in docs/failure-points.md with its edge function. The named proof ``seon.agent-test/a-refused-exit-watch-answers-its-waiter`` is not written (no deftest of that name in test/)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-refused-exit-watch-answers-its-waiter}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-proof-child-that-died-says-why"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the early EOF on the second child's fifo is not reproduced (42 clean runs; its test was removed); Optimization decides to find it from the next red's message or to retire it. 2026-11-08: seon.agent-test/a-two-child-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent was removed (owner: remove tests that are not good; it was intermittently red and booted a root for 14 s; seon.agent-test/a-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent proves a proof run ends with its agent); a proof for this task is a new, small reproduction of the EOF. Seen again 2026-11-07 once slow tests ran last in a merge, with the order kept: b start, a start, a opened, reader opened-a, reader opened-b, a flushed, reader read-a 49, reader read-b -1, b opened, b flushed. So the reader's open of B's fifo returned and read EOF before B logged its open. One process, a fifo opens once and blocks as it should (probed). The test now takes B's start mark as being in its test, so this no longer reds it; the EOF itself is still to find. What was seen: seon.agent-test/a-two-child-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent red once in bin/prove --merge at 2672c71fdc (2026-11-04, target/prove/20261104T130554-11760, shard-1, machine load about 6): began [49 -1], child B's fifo closed with no byte written, before the test killed the agent. The same test at the same digest: green three times alone, and it had been green before. The change under proof (the finished task one-way-to-make-what-an-agent-is-sent, step 2) touches no test child or fifo. What this means: a writer opened the fifo (the reader's open returned) and closed it without its write, so child B died or returned between the FileOutputStream and its write, or its open was not child B's. Inferred, not shown: nothing kept child B's stderr or exit. Seen again on jolt/no-repeats and jolt/no-nudges, and green and red on one tree in three runs at load 5, so the load does not explain it; opening a fifo under Jolt does block with no writer (probed), so the read is a real EOF. To find: what closes the second child's write end before its first byte: seon.agent/run-tests-forked dealing the tests, a child ending early, or a descriptor inherited across the fork. 42 clean runs on a fresh cache did not reproduce it (2026-11-06); the test now keeps what the run answered in its message when began is wrong, so the next red in any prove names the cause: read that first. First such red, 2026-11-07 (bin/prove --merge of 6b5f7dbfc6, its shard-3.log): began [49 -1]; the run answered child A green and child B an error with :jolt/deadlock true, every fiber and thread waiting. That may be B's take that never ends, told after its write, as every run's B would be if not killed first, or a wait before its write; the printed dump is cut there. The byte 50 assertion beside it follows from waiting for the answer. Ruled out by a probe: a reader of a fifo whose writer comes up to 2 s late reads its byte, in either order. Next: print the told fiber's frames whole from the run's :reports. See also Jolt's woken-thread fix (0e4fb237, pinned), which may have closed this: not proved, so read the stage record at its next red. What should be true: a proof child's death in a run says why (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs), so the next red names its cause; then the cause is fixed. Proof: the test, under load, keeps what child B said and how it ended."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reply-is-a-message-with-a-to"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: its plan keeps :seon.message/call, which Context's message-delivery re-model removes; Context rewrites it against that re-model first. Owner, 2026-11-07: reply text becomes a message, and an agent won't always be talking to a person. Today `message-md` is stored as `:seon.model-call/reply` and reaches nobody; a person sees it by watching the page, and `seon.reconcile/tell-tx` tells who started a task only at its end. Correct: the eval tool takes an optional `to`, naming agents by key; `message-md` becomes a message from this agent to them, with no `to` staying on its branch. `chat-message` and `reply-hiccup` become one render: from me is role `assistant`, else `user`, and a reply keeps ``:seon.message/call`` so a provider's pairing with its evals holds. `docs/prompt.md`'s 'your user' changes. Context's first: the request's prefix bytes stay stable across turns. Proof: a chat agent's reply reaches a person's `:messages`; the wire is byte-identical for a transcript with no `to`. See docs/users.md#messages."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-words-are-clojures"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08, yes to a word sweep: names and prose use Clojure's, its libraries' or git's words, or say exactly what happens (docs/vocabulary.md#words-we-do-not-use; docs/decisions.md). Found: take and untaken (seon.agent, seon.system, seon.repl), where git's or Datahike's word for what happens fits; session, which means an agent's env up with a process, on a branch in the db, taking turns (owner, 2026-11-08), so a text that means something else says what it means; {:branch :db} maps, where a point is {:branch :commit :t} (docs/decisions.md, never pass databases); selection, which says nothing (bin/prove deciding which deftests to run); kinds, which become maps chosen by merge (no kind enums). One rename a commit, each with every reader; a stored keyword is renamed by erasing the store, so those wait for the next erase (the content-hashed store, docs/sharing.md#what-a-branchs-nodes-are). Proof: the old names are gone from src, test and docs (git grep), and bin/prove is green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/git-grep-take-untaken-branch-db-maps-other}}
{:seon.task/name "a-real-gate-is-proved-in-seconds"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-10: \"If that test isn't god's gift to the world fucking kill it\": ``seon.agent-test/a-real-gate-judges-a-candidate-once`` (150-174 s median, the largest test) is deleted, and its claim (a prove on a checkout of the candidate, its verdict reaching the branch, the judge reusing it) has no test until this lands, at seconds. Its history: it was the only test of a real gate (a prove on a checkout of the candidate, its verdict reaching the branch, the judge reusing it): seon.fx-test/a-gates-red-runs-again-and-its-green-does-not and seon.agent-test/a-process-test-gets-its-verdict-while-the-agent-can-act stub the gate, so deleting it (a test agent did, reverted) loses the claim. It took about 95 s (median, 2026-11-08; the 247 s was older). Landed ce2c353182: it runs on the shard's root, each shared root on a merges branch of its own (seon.test-store/start-shared-root!), about 8 s fewer in a shard with other agent tests; its digest names the gate code it reaches across root's :gate message (a stopgap for seon.program/a-tests-digest-reads-what-it-ran). Measured 97 s alone after. What is left is the gate's own work on every agent's candidate, measured by hand: the worktree 3 s, bin/build seon 26 s (21 s Jolt's link: the build key covers the whole tree, test/ included, so a candidate that only adds a test rebuilds), the nested plan 16 s (a fresh worktree's program store starts cold), the nested seed 25 s (5,128 forms into a new process store), the shard 7 s. Two ways, each a design change held for the owner: (a) a build key over only what jolt build reads (seon.tree is also what bin/check compares); (b) the gate's program and process stores kept between gate runs beside its main-build slot (a re-seed of a clone measured 3 s, not 25 s; about 35 s off every production gate, not this test's, which starts empty). What stands since (2026-11-10, jolt/tests-cut): the agent's side of a gate that cannot run is seon.agent-test/a-gate-that-cannot-run-is-the-proves-answer (its typed error is the prove's answer; a run that reached no test is a verdict that is not green), and the no-plan refusal held per candidate is seon.fx-test/a-candidate-that-makes-no-plan-is-not-built-again. Its red of 2026-11-10 was a product defect (a gate's checkout ran its root's binary on the main tree's runtime, fixed: docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches), and it read as {:proofs nil} only because its select-keys dropped the typed error my.agent/prove answered: a real-gate test asserts on the whole answer. What has no test: a real checkout's prove reaching the branch. Proof, once (a) or (b) makes it seconds: a real gate on a one-test candidate, its verdict on the branch and the judge's second ask running nothing, its :ms under 30 s."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-user-agent-lives-on-its-own-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-09: \"I want the user to have a process regardless. Anything that's user focused should be handled by this agent too so separate users don't take down the system\"; \"User should have a branch just like the other agents\"; \"The user isn't modifying anything it's sending the requests to agents\". Stage 4 of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09: Optimization the lifecycle and the branch, Web the user agent's walk and root's transport of it (seon.render/the-user-agent-renders-the-users-view). Correct: each user entity on main has a live process, desired state the reconciler closes like any agent's (spawned at boot and again after a death, recorded as for a task's agent, never a watchdog); it has no task and no turn loop, parks in Flow when idle, counts in max-running and is exempt from task admission (it isn't work). Its branch is made like any agent's and syncs with main by the one rule (seon.merge/a-session-syncs-with-main-unless-private); its process owns its writes there (its UI state), so main stays root's alone. Opening an eval is an :eval with origin :context it sends the agent, which swaps :seon.eval/opened through its ctx on its own branch; seon.system/opened!, :seon.agent/opened on main and seon.agent/untaken-facts go. The renders it asks its agents for are held in its process as derived, evictable data beside @ctx, never in @ctx (a swap! there is a checked write). Two agents whose patterns reach each other each answer with the other's last render. Open with the owner: whether opening an eval on a working agent may wait for its run's request (else its REPL takes :context evals at its parks). Proofs, red first: a user agent is running after boot and again after it is killed; a user agent's death leaves root and other users' pages up; its UI state is on its branch, none on main; an open reaches the agent's branch and its next render; the two agents' mutual renders.\n\nPlan approved by Web for Sean, 2026-11-09 (Optimization; builds after stage 2a): desired state as data, ``:seon.agent/kept`` (optional boolean) written by boot for each user; the reconciler's actions gain one rule: an agent with ``:seon.agent/kept`` and no live process is spawned on its own branch (seon.system/spawn!, the writer's :spawn, no task), its death recorded as any agent's and respawned at the next pass, never a watchdog; it counts in max-running (reconcile room count) and never enters :ready, by its data. Its branch is cut at its first spawn and resumed after (init!'s resumed path, load-diff!). Owner, 2026-11-09: opening an eval is an :eval with origin :context the user agent sends the agent, and the agent's model reads it too; a busy agent's REPL takes :context evals at its parks (await-reply), and until taken the user agent shows the open as its own pending request on its branch; seon.system/opened!, :seon.agent/opened and seon.agent/untaken-facts go (whichever of 1b and this lands second removes untaken-facts). Facts (file:line): the user is already an agent entity (:seon.agent/key plus :seon.user/name, written by task/boot-tx task.clj:325-334); the reconciler reads only tasks (actions reconcile.clj:448); init! tolerates no task (agent.clj:1636). First proof, red first: a taskless agent survives its hello and parks. Estimate about +40 -25."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/user-agent-running-after-boot-again-after-killed}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-runs-measures-are-their-own-entities"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-09: re-model 1, yes: each run's measures are their own entity with a ref to what they measured, summed when read. Step 1 (a function's cost) is built on jolt/sync: ``:seon.run/id`` (unique per write), ``:seon.run/of``, ``:seon.run/agent``, ``:seon.run/stats``, summed by ``seon.agent/fn-cost``; :seon.fn/stats gone. Step 2, not built, lands with the Context session: a test's results as runs (``:seon.run/digest``, status, failures, ticks, bytes); a test's verdict is its newest run at its current digest, flaky being two statuses at one digest. Writers: seon.proofs' test-facts, facts-worth-keeping, result-tx and seon.agent/record-results!. Readers to move: seon.agent's run-tests-forked, with-gate-verdicts and judge!, the task pattern's :proved pull, ctx/status (Context's) and render/ui's proofs-green count (Web's). Deletes the five :seon.test/* attributes on the var and seon.proofs' flaky/sum merge logic. About 80-120 lines; a store erase."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/test-s-verdict-newest-seon-run-entity-current}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-done-counts-only-the-reds-its-change-reaches"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-10 (Context's baseline of three Sonnet agents on main f4968a8624, its agent-base worktree root; the run's path is in the managing session's notes folder): an agent's own proofs went green, and its task was closed :closed on two reds it never touched, seon.program-test/a-test-that-starts-a-process-reaches-the-program-it-runs and seon.render-test/a-render-reaches-no-process (over their 4B-tick budget inside a big prove, green alone: task seon.proofs/a-tests-ticks-are-the-same-alone-and-in-a-run). The class: a done rule that charges an agent with a red its change does not reach, so any stray red on main closes every task. What correct looks like (managing session, 2026-11-10): a red counts against a task only when the task names the test as a proof (seon.task/proof?) or the branch's change reaches it, its reach digest on the branch differing from base's (what the plan already stores: seon.agent/test-plan's digests against base's); a red at a digest equal to base's is main's red, told to its area as a task (seon.prove/a-red-in-the-daily-full-test-is-a-task) and never closes the agent's task. Read the judge path first (seon.agent's judge and done rule, seon.task/proof?, seon.reconcile's end of a run) and say in the landing what the rule replaces. Proof, red first: a world where base holds a red test the branch's change does not reach and the task's proof is green: the task merges; the same red reached by the change: it does not."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/world-where-base-holds-red-test-branch-s}}
 {:seon.task/name "each-eval-is-its-own-commit-is-a-proof-that-can-fail"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 by Optimization (fixing its flake, jolt/evalflake 9ccfcc76c4: the walk took (first (parent-commit-ids db)), a set, and at a merge followed main's parent): seon.agent-test/an-agent-commits-after-each-eval stays green when seon.turn/evaluated! drops its per-eval commit, because the scripted turn makes one model eval a turn. Correct: the turn makes two evals in one model call, so a dropped per-eval commit shows as one commit for two evals. Proof: red under that mutation."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-request-costs-the-same-however-many-came-before"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by Optimization (2026-11-10, measuring main's content mark, docs/storage.md#mains-content-mark): one agent answering an outside eval of 1 through root's server (bin/ask), 15 requests a round, three rounds, load 12-18: with the mark the p50 went 100 -> 122 -> 122 ms; with the mark's skip off (seon.merge/same-content? false) it went 152 -> 199 -> 220 ms. The second growth is understood: the merge base never moves while only records move main, so merge-main!'s seon.db/branch-base walk and the content diffs span every commit since; the mark removes it. The first, ~20 ms over 45 requests with the mark, is not explained. Suspects: something per request kept on the agent's branch and read whole each turn (its evals, model calls, the page's walk over the conversation), or main's records since the base read by a reader that is not the content diff (seon.agent/untaken-facts, undelivered). Measure first: the request's ticks at request 10, 100 and 300 on one agent (seon.profile/measure! inside the agent around seon.turn/outside-reply!), each phase as in notes writer-latency-2026-11-10.md; then the cause, and a cost proof that holds a request flat in the requests before it. Proof: seon.agent-test/an-agents-request-costs-the-same-however-many-came-before."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agents-request-costs-the-same-however-many-came-before}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-fiber-error-reaches-its-reply"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization (first draft by Deep fixes, 2026-11-11). What exists: an eval's own throw comes back in its reply as :ex (```seon.repl/wrap-caught```). A go block, a fiber or a thread the eval STARTED that throws after the eval's form returned has no caller: until Jolt c3742a8a its error was printed to the agent process's stderr and the agent never saw it, so an agent that starts a go loop which dies reads a reply that says nothing went wrong. Jolt now hands every such error to the handler a program sets with ``Thread/setDefaultUncaughtExceptionHandler`` (a thread's, a go block's, a fiber body's, a fiber-execute task's, a transducer's completion; docs/forks.md#jolt), and root sets one for its own process (the :platform problem on main). Correct: the agent process sets the same handler, and an error it receives becomes a fact on the agent's entity that the agent reads at its next request: which eval started the body when that is known (the eval whose form was running when the body was spawned), else the agent itself, with the error as data (docs/errors.md). No new attribute until the pull has been tried at a REPL with the form the agent's page would use; an existing mechanism first: a message from the system entity. Not this task: an error inside the eval's own form (already :ex). Proof: seon.agent-test/an-evals-fiber-error-reaches-its-reply: an eval starts a go block that throws after the form returns; the agent's next request holds the error and names the eval."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-evals-fiber-error-reaches-its-reply}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-names-the-agent-it-continues"
  :seon.task/plan "Waits on the owner's choice (a session is an agent, or an entity of its own: the two shapes as forms are in the session notes, 2026-11-11); built the day he says the first. What exists, read: a fork makes a sibling task named with a -fork- suffix and a number, copies the plan and proofs, and starts an agent told at its start which agent it is a clone of (```seon.server/fork!```); that is an argument of the start and is stored nowhere, so afterwards only the task's name says where the fork came from. Who started an agent is stored (the starter's agents), and where its branch was cut (its base commit); whose conversation it goes on from is not. Gate, why the existing refs cannot hold it: the starter of a fork may be a person or the system, not the agent forked; a task's example sessions are sessions to show whoever works the task, not what this agent continues. Plan: one ref on the agent, to the agent whose conversation it continues, written where a start is given one (the fork; a go-back; a rerun that should read the run before it). The name is settled at a REPL first. Its readers, as forms on a pulled agent c: the agent it came from, (get c the-ref); a fork's whole conversation, the source's members up to the fork's base and then its own, by one join to a depth in the agent's pattern; a reflect fork's subject, the same ref (so a reflect the system starts needs nothing else); the forks of an agent, the ref reversed. A rerun finds the run before it by order among the task's agents and needs this ref only if it should read that run's conversation. Deletes: the -fork- and number in a task's name as the only trace, and the clone-of argument as a fact nobody can read later. Proof, red first: an agent forked from another holds the ref, its pull reaches the source's evals before the cut and none after, and the source's pull lists its forks."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-branch-reads-its-tasks-execution-from-main"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime's one-writer design (deps found it). A task's :seon.task/execution has one writer, root, on main; an agent's branch reads it from main as heard (``seon.agent/heard-main``), never a copy it writes. Tried as 053ed1cade (```seon.agent/begin-run!``` merging main down when main moved or the branch lacked the task) and reverted: attach! spawns the agent before it writes the release on main, so begin-run! could run first and the branch read :held (seon.server-test/an-agent-reads-its-task-running, red by timing). Swarm's merge tasks also ended behind 12-15 on that branch, but a probe showed no agent called merge-main! at all and the red stayed after the revert, so swarm is a separate cause (unread). Today the branch writes :running at begin-run! (two writers of one key). Decided inside the one-writer design and the agent's env (design one-unit-spec, Write): my.task/current and the task part of @ctx read execution from main as heard, and begin-run-tx writes none. See `docs/agent.md#merging`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/an-agent-reads-its-task-running}}

 {:seon.task/name "every-stateful-process-is-a-flow-agent-in-one-tree-with-ports-named-by-message-keys"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner rulings: one unified design with duplicates hunted down; the Flow way; root is not special, it is the first agent; resources made at startup and released at shutdown; types are schemas; port names fully namespaced. Already landed (fork-one steps 1-2 and keys-schemas): jolt.async.remote.flow/fork! closing inherited streams, test children as procs of their agent's graph by fork!, the agent rows (:seon.agent/parent, branch, commit, t and the four options model-turns, render-walk, interrupts, keeps-branch), :seon.process/exit and end, the link's two unions, a request's address. Plan only what is left. Design: an agent forks its own children directly (single-threaded, :io procs only); root forks nothing after boot, holds the :mixed procs (the one writer, flusher, render, fx workers) and forks through its template child (declared {:forks-through :template}). Ports are the message's schema key: :seon.writer/request, notice, commit; :seon.step/request, answer, event, control (:interrupt in the enum); :seon.process/end; :seon.link/down and up. Fixed buffers for everything acted on; promise-chan only in edge-request; only observer ports (events window, taps, web streams, render's commit, SSE) may slide or drop. Remaining work, in order: (1) clones, the fork server as a Flow graph, root's template child, :seon.process/end and the stop sequence (idle stops at once; busy with interrupts true gets :interrupt then stops; interrupts false is killed; the stall check is the one clock); a child's parent records its exit, an orphan records its own end. (2) one-ask: seon.step/request, take-reply, edge-request, routed up by :seon.step/to, replacing pend/ask/answered/ask-edge, await-reply, the fork server's :waiters, the api's, reconciler's, prove's, vector's, render's and web's pend tables and plain promises. (3) one-wait-on-main: every :main reader onto :seon.writer/commit in order, await-db an edge-request to the api holding each wait, answered :seon.system/no-agent at stop. (4) one-writer in root on :seon.writer/request, senders resolve tempids (seon.db/ids!), schema checked per branch in the writer, catch-up merges recorded :seon.tx/merged. (5) flow-children and unit-fast (warm child at most 5 ms). Deletes: fd passing, end-with!/SIGIO, flow-stop-ms, run-loop, the agents' writers and intake. Proof, red first: after its first turn on a main that moved by a non-conflicting commit, a merge task's branch holds a catch-up merge with main's head and no merge-main! call; with a conflict, the conflict is the next turn's error. Checks: ports named by schema key, no blocking take in a step, one form per family."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.step/every-seon-proc-follows-one-flow-standard-with-a-runnable-example-each"}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-of-main-applies-every-change-main-made"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-14 (context): `seon.agent/merge-main!` on the fixed main moved the first task's branch base to main's head and applied 0 of main's changes, so a var main deleted and a changed file stay stale; the base moves only when `seon.merge/delta-three-way` saw changes (agent.clj:562). Measured (runtime): one merge of main costs 10.26B ticks and 52.2 GB: the tx is transacted twice (speculated whole to read the registry) and 1,028 forms are reloaded at ~29 MB each. Fix correctness first, then cost: the merge applies every change or keeps it unmerged, never moving the base past what it did not apply; `seon.task/carry-tx` goes, so it is one three-way merge (data); it runs on main's program; lever: re-fork from the warm main and load only the branch's diff. Estimate written first. Owners: runtime, data."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-merge-of-a-moved-main-applies-what-it-changed seon.merge-cost-test/a-merge-costs-what-moved seon.merge-test/a-branch-cut-before-a-deletion-merges-the-deletion}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-child-starts-on-its-branchs-environment"
  :seon.task/plan "Plan Stage 0.5; owner 2026-11-14: bootstrap checks once, runtime guarantees, no guards. Today a test child is a fork of root's agent and carries root's config, environment, heap, threads and libraries, patched piecewise by `seon.fx/checkout-env!` and ``seon.config/hold-checkout!`` (deleted since) (agent.clj:1254). Fix: hold-env! is every started process's first act (agent init, test child start, root boot); it brings program and config in place and refuses a pin, native library or binary mismatch as :seon.process/env-mismatch, reported as a platform problem, never a verdict; a child for another environment is spawned on that branch, never forked across. Reads resources/seon/environment.edn (deps). Deletes checkout-env! and hold-checkout!. Design: runtime and tests, plans/runtime-tests-fork-on-env.md. Owners: runtime, tests."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-child-whose-config-differs-from-its-branch-is-refused-and-stores-no-verdict seon.agent-test/a-test-child-of-a-branch-reads-its-branchs-config}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
]
